WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.276 --> 00:00:07.519
Hello, everyone!

2
00:00:15.146 --> 00:00:22.312
Welcome back to Superstars in History—the fashionable talk show with famous people from the past.

3
00:00:22.892 --> 00:00:24.594
I'm your host, Faye Moss.

4
00:00:25.334 --> 00:00:33.141
Today's guest played a big part in changing women's lives by changing women's clothes and creating some looks

5
00:00:33.181 --> 00:00:34.923
that are still popular today.

6
00:00:35.823 --> 00:00:45.170
Straight from history, here is fashion legend and businesswoman Gabrielle "Coco" Chanel!

7
00:00:56.038 --> 00:00:57.019
Thank you, thank you.

8
00:00:57.359 --> 00:01:04.587
Merci. Welcome to the show, Gabrielle—or should I call you Coco?

9
00:01:05.268 --> 00:01:06.408
Call me Coco, Faye.

10
00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:10.092
How did you get the name Coco?

11
00:01:10.652 --> 00:01:12.193
Is that what your parents called you?

12
00:01:13.414 --> 00:01:19.099
No. My parents well, I barely knew my father, and my mother died when I was 11, in

13
00:01:19.379 --> 00:01:24.944
1895. I was put in a convent in central France where nuns took care of girls with no

14
00:01:24.984 --> 00:01:29.568
families. I learned how to sew from the nuns there, which became quite a useful skill!

15
00:01:30.876 --> 00:01:35.797
As for the name Coco, after I turned 18, I was too old to stay at the convent.

16
00:01:36.758 --> 00:01:39.598
I moved to a different town and worked as a seamstress by day.

17
00:01:40.099 --> 00:01:44.620
However, I really wanted to be a performer, so I sang at a café at night.

18
00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:48.341
I became popular by singing the song "Who Has Seen Coco?"

19
00:01:50.841 --> 00:01:52.762
Soon people were calling me by that name!

20
00:01:53.902 --> 00:01:57.603
Wow, you were a singer before you were a fashion icon?

21
00:01:57.983 --> 00:02:00.404
You must have been really talented.

22
00:02:00.992 --> 00:02:02.333
Why don't you sing for us?

23
00:02:04.294 --> 00:02:04.834
No, no.

24
00:02:05.635 --> 00:02:07.376
I actually didn't have much talent!

25
00:02:08.136 --> 00:02:12.298
By my early twenties, I realized that my singing voice wasn't good enough for me to become a

26
00:02:12.338 --> 00:02:18.101
professional. How did you get started in the world of fashion?

27
00:02:19.222 --> 00:02:21.883
I was poor and needed a way to make a better living.

28
00:02:22.524 --> 00:02:25.525
Luckily, I'd made the acquaintance of some rich society people.

29
00:02:26.186 --> 00:02:30.348
Since I'd already been making hats for fun, I decided to turn it into a business.

30
00:02:31.144 --> 00:02:36.267
I was able to set up my own shop in 1910, with some financial help from my wealthy

31
00:02:36.327 --> 00:02:40.789
friends. My hat designs quickly became popular with some famous actresses.

32
00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:43.671
What made them so popular?

33
00:02:45.252 --> 00:02:46.192
They were simple!

34
00:02:47.033 --> 00:02:52.456
Hats at the time were complex, heavy creations, with flowers and bows and frill.

35
00:02:53.216 --> 00:02:55.777
These ornate designs weighed women down.

36
00:02:56.398 --> 00:02:58.919
All of women's fashion in the 1900s and 1910s was like this.

37
00:03:00.928 --> 00:03:05.912
Women wore tight corsets with bone or steel that were uncomfortable and hard to move in.

38
00:03:06.592 --> 00:03:10.896
Dresses were tight and had many layers—the horror of it all!

39
00:03:11.776 --> 00:03:15.639
In fact, one evening I wore a dress that was so tight that I had to loosen it.

40
00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:19.162
But then I couldn't fasten it again, and I had nothing to cover up with!

41
00:03:21.224 --> 00:03:24.646
It sounds like women's fashion was really inconvenient.

42
00:03:25.807 --> 00:03:26.588
Oui, yes!

43
00:03:27.232 --> 00:03:32.657
That's why, when I opened my first clothing boutique in 1913, I wanted to make clothes that were

44
00:03:32.737 --> 00:03:33.918
comfortable for women.

45
00:03:34.618 --> 00:03:38.942
I designed simple skirts, jackets, and blouses using light fabrics.

46
00:03:39.603 --> 00:03:44.067
Women could easily move, enjoy sports, and even ride horses in these clothes.

47
00:03:44.767 --> 00:03:48.010
Back then, horseback riding was a favorite activity of mine.

48
00:03:48.891 --> 00:03:52.234
Best of all, women didn't have to wear corsets while wearing the clothing.

49
00:03:52.734 --> 00:03:54.716
Two years later I opened a second store.

50
00:03:55.284 --> 00:03:57.966
And during World War I, my sales increased.

51
00:03:59.367 --> 00:04:00.508
That's surprising.

52
00:04:00.968 --> 00:04:04.951
Why did your clothes continue to be popular even during wartime?

53
00:04:06.072 --> 00:04:09.294
Fashion, is place and time, Faye.

54
00:04:10.154 --> 00:04:15.038
My designs were right for the time—subdued and simple, not fancy and fussy.

55
00:04:15.838 --> 00:04:21.382
I admit, I was inspired by military uniforms too, so my designs were in plain colors and functional.

56
00:04:22.423 --> 00:04:27.410
In a time as dreadful as war, when young men were dying daily, no one wanted to dress

57
00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:29.492
in the showy prewar style.

58
00:04:30.192 --> 00:04:31.813
After the war, fashion changed again.

59
00:04:32.634 --> 00:04:36.856
Women, including me, began bobbing their hair and wearing shorter skirts.

60
00:04:38.017 --> 00:04:39.898
That was in the 1920s, right?

61
00:04:40.819 --> 00:04:48.084
Around that time, you began creating a look that's still popular today, which many people call a timeless—

62
00:04:48.924 --> 00:04:49.765
You mean this suit?

63
00:04:51.806 --> 00:04:56.973
Men, of course, had worn suits for a long time, and even I wore men's suits occasionally—they were

64
00:04:57.053 --> 00:04:57.934
very comfortable.

65
00:04:57.954 --> 00:05:00.955
"Why didn't women have an equivalent garment?"

66
00:05:01.015 --> 00:05:01.495
I wondered.

67
00:05:02.456 --> 00:05:07.078
So, I decided to make one—a comfortable matching jacket and skirt.

68
00:05:07.818 --> 00:05:13.040
I chose to use wool or tweed since I visited England and Scotland often, and I'd noticed that

69
00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:15.801
men's sporting suits were often made with those materials.

70
00:05:16.482 --> 00:05:21.324
To be sure that women felt comfortable in my suits, I didn't just take their measurements before making

71
00:05:21.344 --> 00:05:21.844
the clothing.

72
00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:28.443
I watched my clients move as they would in their everyday lives sitting down, opening doors, getting into

73
00:05:28.503 --> 00:05:30.163
cars, walking up stairs.

74
00:05:31.164 --> 00:05:33.585
It sounds like you thought of everything!

75
00:05:33.605 --> 00:05:36.846
No wonder your suit became such a classic.

76
00:05:37.506 --> 00:05:39.467
Yes, it was very popular.

77
00:05:40.348 --> 00:05:45.610
I closed my shops during World War II, and I didn't return to designing clothing for quite some

78
00:05:45.650 --> 00:05:48.171
time. I was busy working on other things.

79
00:05:49.111 --> 00:05:53.862
Finally, in 1954, I had my first fashion show in 15 years.

80
00:05:58.846 --> 00:06:04.870
But by then many new male designers were working in the industry, and the latest designs for women

81
00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:08.233
featured full, heavy skirts and tight waists.

82
00:06:09.834 --> 00:06:12.776
I did not like this "New Look," as it was called.

83
00:06:13.537 --> 00:06:17.760
I had made clothes freer for women, and the postwar styles were restrictive again.

84
00:06:18.792 --> 00:06:23.436
My new collection featured the simple, elegant suits and dresses I had always made.

85
00:06:24.658 --> 00:06:32.325
The French press wasn't very kind about my show—they said that the new Chanel line was outdated, unchanged

86
00:06:32.445 --> 00:06:33.606
since before the war.

87
00:06:34.326 --> 00:06:38.250
But American fashion editors promoted my designs in their magazines.

88
00:06:39.551 --> 00:06:44.516
Your designs definitely changed fashion, and many are still popular today.

89
00:06:45.312 --> 00:06:49.013
I wonder what women would be wearing today without your influence.

90
00:06:49.713 --> 00:06:52.494
Thank you very much, Coco, for being on the show.

91
00:06:53.014 --> 00:06:55.235
We'll let you get back to history now.

92
00:06:56.375 --> 00:06:57.356
Au revoir, Faye!

93
00:07:02.197 --> 00:07:07.919
Thank you, everyone, for joining my interview with the legendary fashion designer Coco Chanel.

94
00:07:08.699 --> 00:07:17.342
After her comeback in 1954, Coco Chanel continued to work, cutting fabric for her designs herself.

95
00:07:17.482 --> 00:07:21.926
In 1971 she died in Paris at the age of 87.

96
00:07:22.827 --> 00:07:29.033
The company with her name is still famous, and her original Paris boutique and workshop are still in

97
00:07:29.113 --> 00:07:35.879
operation. I hope you enjoyed the show, and I'll see you next week with another surprise guest from

98
00:07:35.939 --> 00:07:36.500
the past.

99
00:07:37.268 --> 00:07:41.211
You've been watching Faye Moss on Superstars in History!
